# ============================================================================
# honeypot-cti-report — .gitignore
#
# Rule of thumb: this repo may contain CODE and ANALYSIS.
# It may never contain: secrets or raw honeypot traffic (attacker-supplied data).
# ============================================================================

# --- 1. Secrets: private keys, tokens, env files ---------------------------
# If any of these is ever committed, deleting them in a later commit is NOT
# enough — git retains history forever. Rotate the key, then rewrite history.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.pub.lock
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
.ssh/
secrets/
credentials*

# --- 2. Raw collected data: large, and full of attacker-supplied PII -------
# Attackers type usernames, passwords and hostnames into the honeypot.
# Those strings stay in data/raw/ and never enter git. Only *derived* and
# *sanitized* data in data/processed/ is committed.
data/raw/
logs/
*.log
cowrie.json
cowrie.log
tty.log

# --- 3. Attacker uploads: never open, never execute, never commit ----------
# Whatever the honeypot captured from a compromised "host" is hostile input.
# Analysis hashes these files; it does not read or store their contents.
share/
downloads/
*.pickle

# --- 4. Cowrie state directories -------------------------------------------
# Defensive only. This project analyses published logs and does not run a
# honeypot, so these should never appear. They are listed so that a stray local
# Cowrie install cannot leak state or SSH host keys into the repository.
my-honeypot/
cowrie-env/
cowrie.cfg
cowrie.cfg.dist
var/

# --- 5. Python --------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- 6. macOS / editor noise ------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
._*
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/